        "Hidetoshi Katori",
        "Jun Ye"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks have transformed precision timekeeping and enable exceptionally sensitive tests and measurements.",
        "attribution": "Katori's lattice-clock concept and Ye's experimental development are complementary and central.",
        "maturity": "Independent laboratories have demonstrated extraordinary performance and a substantial scientific programme.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The eventual practical redefinition of the second remains a collective metrological process."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B006",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Federico Capasso",
        "Jérôme Faist",
        "Alfred Y. Cho"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The quantum cascade laser was a genuinely new semiconductor light source with major scientific and technological reach.",
        "attribution": "Faist and Capasso made the defining invention; Cho's foundational heterostructure work merits serious consideration.",
        "maturity": "Three decades of applications and continuing development leave no doubt about its impact.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The precise boundary between enabling semiconductor technology and the prize-worthy invention requires scrutiny."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B013",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Paul B. Corkum",
        "Maciej Lewenstein",
        "Kenneth J. Schafer"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The recollision picture and quantum theory supplied the physical basis for high-harmonic generation and attosecond science.",
        "attribution": "Corkum, Lewenstein and Schafer made closely connected foundational theoretical contributions.",
        "maturity": "The framework is deeply embedded in a mature experimental field and has repeatedly proved predictive.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "A recent prize for attosecond pulse methods may constrain timing and require a sharp distinction of the theoretical contribution."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B019",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Sheperd S. Doeleman",
        "Heino Falcke",
        "J. Anton Zensus"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Black-hole shadow imaging was an extraordinary instrumental and observational achievement, directly probing gravity near event horizons.",
        "attribution": "The named figures represent key conceptual, network-building and observational leadership, but the achievement is profoundly collaborative.",
        "maturity": "The first image has been reinforced by further targets, polarimetry and continuing analysis.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Individual attribution within the Event Horizon Telescope collaboration is unusually difficult."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B014",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Gerald Gabrielse",
        "Jeffrey Hangst",
        "Stefan Ulmer"
